// wrap-pagebreak-demo — a wrapped figure stays whole across a page break.
//
// A wrapfigure anchored near the bottom of a page would, if the page broke between its lines, leave the figure on
// one page and the text it narrows on the next. The engine forbids a page break inside a wrap band, so the whole
// wrap — figure and the lines beside it — moves to the next page together; and a figure that shipped with its page
// stops narrowing the lines of later pages. This document runs past one page so the second figure, anchored where
// the first page has run out of room, is forced whole onto page two.

\use{document}
\use{float}

\title {Wrapped Figures Across Page Breaks}
\author {the float package}
\maketitle

\section{A wrap that fits on the page}

\wrapfigure{r}{1.7in}{
  \picture width:1.7in height:1.1in {
    \fill{lightblue}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 121 78}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \caption{This figure fits here.}
}

A wrapped figure anchors against the margin where it is written and the following lines flow around it in a narrowed
measure. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et
dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea
commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla
pariatur. Once the lines clear the bottom of the figure they return to the full measure and run flush again.

